  "verbalization": "Tobramycin, a broad-spectrum antibiotic, is linked to Uveitis through a chain of indications that involve Iris Disease. The evidence suggests that Tobramycin may be repurposed for treating Uveitis via its ability to influence Iris Disease, which in turn affects the pathophysiology of Uveitis. The visible paths indicate that Tobramycin is associated with Iris Disease, and this condition is linked to Uveitis through the use of Rimexolone, Medrysone, and Fluorometholone—three corticosteroids that are known to have anti-inflammatory effects. These corticosteroids are often used in the treatment of Uveitis, suggesting a potential mechanism where Tobramycin could exert a similar effect through a shared pathway involving Iris Disease. The integration of these paths highlights a possible role for Tobramycin in Uveitis treatment through its interaction with corticosteroid pathways, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated."
